      {
        "id": "lem-qft-lattice-local-gauss-flux",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Region charge equals boundary electric flux",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q5; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/lem-qft-lattice-local-gauss-flux.md",
        "design_state": "reserved claim; complete conditional argument authored; Step-1 readiness escalated pending batch-37 carrier",
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data",
          "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the data of [[def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data]] and let $R\\subseteq V$. Define\n$$\\partial R=\\{e\\in E:\\text{exactly one endpoint of }e\\text{ lies in }R\\},$$\nwith $\\epsilon(e,R)=1$ when $s(e)\\in R,t(e)\\notin R$ and $\\epsilon(e,R)=-1$ when $t(e)\\in R,s(e)\\notin R$. On the domain\n$$\\mathcal D_R=\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}\\cap\\bigcap_{e\\in\\partial R}D(E_e),$$\nthe region number $N_R=\\sum_{x\\in R}(n_x-b_xI)$ obeys the exact identity\n$$N_R\\psi=\\left(\\sum_{e\\in\\partial R}\\epsilon(e,R)E_e\\right)\\psi\\qquad(\\psi\\in\\mathcal D_R).$$\nThus for $R=V$, where $\\partial R=\\varnothing$, every physical vector has total occupation $\\sum_x n_x=\\sum_x b_x$. This finite-graph identity is exact and makes no continuum-limit claim.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979)",
              "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 55-page review; relevant Hamiltonian and Gauss-law treatment in §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696. The Rome1 PDF is byte-identical to the previously retrieved full text."
            },
            {
              "title": "Patrick Emonts and Erez Zohar, Gauss Law, Minimal Coupling and Fermionic PEPS for Lattice Gauge Theories, SciPost Physics Lecture Notes 12 (2020)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1807.01294",
              "locator": "Full-text arXiv HTML rendition; §§2.1–2.4, printed pp. 3–8. The corresponding complete 32-page PDF was also retrieved and read."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-lattice-ward-identities",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Exact regulated gauge and infinitesimal Ward identities",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q6; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/lem-qft-lattice-ward-identities.md",
        "design_state": "reserved claim; complete conditional argument authored; Step-1 readiness escalated pending batch-37 carrier",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data",
          "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection",
          "lem-qft-lattice-physical-evolution",
          "def-axiom-of-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-lattice-physical-evolution": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the finite-graph model of [[def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data]] and the Axiom of Choice. Let $\\psi\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$ be a normalized vector. A bounded operator $O$ has gauge charge $q=(q_x)_{x\\in V}\\in\\mathbb Z^V$ when\n$$U(\\alpha)OU(\\alpha)^\\dagger=e^{i\\sum_x q_x\\alpha_x}O.$$\nThen $\\langle\\psi,O\\psi\\rangle=0$ whenever $q\\ne0$, and any finite product of bounded gauge-charge eigenoperators has zero expectation when the sum of its charge vectors is nonzero.\n\nFor each $x$, if $O$ maps $D(G_x)$ into itself and $[G_x,O]$ extends to a bounded operator, then\n$$\\langle\\psi,[G_x,O]\\psi\\rangle=0.$$\nThe same assertions apply to $O(t)=\\mathcal U(t)^\\dagger O\\mathcal U(t)$: the finite-regulator time evolution preserves the gauge charge and the domains $D(G_x)$. These are exact operator identities for the stated regulator; no trace or path-integral identity and no continuum Ward–Takahashi statement is asserted.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979)",
              "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 55-page review; relevant Hamiltonian and Gauss-law treatment in §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696. The Rome1 PDF is byte-identical to the previously retrieved full text."
            },
            {
              "title": "Patrick Emonts and Erez Zohar, Gauss Law, Minimal Coupling and Fermionic PEPS for Lattice Gauge Theories, SciPost Physics Lecture Notes 12 (2020)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1807.01294",
              "locator": "Full-text arXiv HTML rendition; §§2.1–2.4, printed pp. 3–8. The corresponding complete 32-page PDF was also retrieved and read."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-qed-gauss-localization",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Gauss law and charged localization obstruction",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q6; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/lem-qft-qed-gauss-localization.md",
        "design_state": "reserved claim; complete conditional argument authored; Step-1 readiness escalated pending batch-37 carrier",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "lem-qft-lattice-local-gauss-flux"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-qft-lattice-local-gauss-flux": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the exact finite-graph model and let $R\\subseteq V$. Set $Q_R=\\sum_{x\\in R}(n_x-b_xI)$ and let $\\partial R$ and its orientation signs be as in [[lem-qft-lattice-local-gauss-flux]]. On $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}\\cap\\bigcap_{e\\in\\partial R}D(E_e)$, suppose a bounded operator $O$ preserves $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$ and, for every boundary link $e$, maps $D(E_e)$ into itself and satisfies $E_eO\\phi=OE_e\\phi$ for every $\\phi\\in D(E_e)$. Then $[Q_R,O]\\psi=0$ for every vector in that physical domain. Consequently, a charge-changing operator with $[Q_R,O]\\psi=qO\\psi$ for a fixed $q\\ne0$ cannot both preserve the physical sector and commute with the boundary electric operators while acting nontrivially on any such physical vector.\n\nThere is also the following conditional continuum statement; it does not assert that a continuum representation exists. Let $D$ be a common invariant dense domain in a Hilbert space, and let $\\rho(f)$ and $E_k(g_k)$ be linear assignments from real-valued test functions $f,g_k\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ to operators $D\\to D$. For a real vector test function $\\mathbf g=(g_1,g_2,g_3)\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ set $E(\\mathbf g)=\\sum_{k=1}^3E_k(g_k)$. Assume the assignments satisfy on $D$\n$$\\rho(f)=-\\varepsilon_0\\sum_{k=1}^3E_k(\\partial_k f)\\quad(f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)),$$\nfor a fixed $\\varepsilon_0>0$, and let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be compact. Suppose $O$ is bounded, maps $D$ into itself, and satisfies $E(\\mathbf g)O\\psi=OE(\\mathbf g)\\psi$ for every $\\psi\\in D$ whenever $\\operatorname{supp}\\mathbf g$ is disjoint from $K$. For each integer $R\\ge R_0$, choose a real-valued $\\chi_R\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ equal to $1$ on a neighborhood of $K$ and with $\\operatorname{supp}\\nabla\\chi_R$ disjoint from $K$, and put $Q_R=\\rho(\\chi_R)=-\\varepsilon_0 E(\\nabla\\chi_R)$. Assume there is an operator $Q:D\\to D$ such that, for every $\\psi\\in D$, both $Q_R\\psi\\to Q\\psi$ and $Q_RO\\psi\\to QO\\psi$ as integer $R\\to\\infty$. Then $[Q,O]\\psi=0$ on $D$. A bounded charge-changing operator satisfying $[Q,O]\\psi=qO\\psi$ for $q\\ne0$ on $D$ must therefore be the zero operator. These continuum conclusions are conditional on the displayed domain, locality, convergence and commutator-passage assumptions.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979)",
              "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 55-page review; relevant Hamiltonian and Gauss-law treatment in §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696. The Rome1 PDF is byte-identical to the previously retrieved full text."
            },
