              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1807.01294",
              "locator": "Full-text arXiv HTML rendition; §§2.1–2.4, printed pp. 3–8. The corresponding complete 32-page PDF was also retrieved and read."
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-lattice-ward-contact",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Time-ordered Ward contact relation",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/workers/qed/proofs.md#Q6; reserved proposed inventory, exact conditional statement authored in items/lem-qft-lattice-ward-contact.md",
        "design_state": "reserved claim; complete conditional argument authored; Step-1 readiness escalated pending batch-37 carrier",
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data",
          "lem-qft-lattice-ward-identities",
          "lem-qft-lattice-physical-evolution",
          "def-axiom-of-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-lattice-ward-identities": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-lattice-physical-evolution": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the compact finite-graph model and the Axiom of Choice. Fix a vertex $x$, pairwise distinct real times $t_1,\\ldots,t_n$ with $n\\ge1$, bounded operators $O_j$, and a normalized physical vector $\\psi$. Assume each $O_j$ maps $D(G_x)$ into itself and has a bounded commutator with $G_x$. Let\n$$O_j(t_j)=\\mathcal U(t_j)^\\dagger O_j\\mathcal U(t_j),\\qquad G_x(t)=\\mathcal U(t)^\\dagger G_x\\mathcal U(t),$$\nwhere $\\mathcal U(t)=e^{-itH/\\hbar}$. Define ordinary time ordering by placing the factor with the larger time to the left; $G_x$ is bosonic, so moving it past an insertion introduces no grading sign. Then $G_x(t)=G_x$, and, as a distribution in $t$,\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\left\\langle\\psi,\\mathcal T\\!\\left[G_x(t)\\prod_{j=1}^nO_j(t_j)\\right]\\psi\\right\\rangle=\\sum_{j=1}^n\\delta(t-t_j)\\left\\langle\\psi,\\mathcal T\\!\\left([G_x,O_j(t_j)]\\prod_{i\\ne j}O_i(t_i)\\right)\\psi\\right\\rangle.$$\nThe products and commutators are evaluated on the common domains just specified. This is a finite-regulator contact identity, not a claim about continuum renormalized time-ordered products.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John B. Kogut, An Introduction to Lattice Gauge Theory and Spin Systems, Reviews of Modern Physics 51 (1979)",
              "url": "https://chimera.roma1.infn.it/ENZO/FC/ESTRATTI/DUALITY/kogut1979.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 55-page review; relevant Hamiltonian and Gauss-law treatment in §VI.C, printed pp. 694–696. The Rome1 PDF is byte-identical to the previously retrieved full text."
